message.content:
On the lateral chest X-ray, there is increased opacity in the posterior lower lung zone with obscuration of the posterior aspect of the left hemidiaphragm and retrocardiac density. The major fissure appears displaced inferiorly and posteriorly on the left side. These findings are characteristic of left lower lobe atelectasis (loss of volume and increased density behind the heart). Right lower lobe atelectasis would typically show opacity overlying the spine with preservation of the left hemidiaphragm contour and different fissure displacement.

Uncertainty: Single lateral view limits definitive confirmation without PA correlation, but retrocardiac opacity strongly favors LLL.

{"answer":"A. left lower lobe pulmonary atelectasis","reason":"Retrocardiac opacity with obscured posterior left hemidiaphragm on lateral view indicates left lower lobe collapse."}

